AB  - Modern edge intelligence systems execute multiple LLM agentic workflows concurrently, where each workflow is modelled as a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) of heterogeneous LLM inference tasks with data dependencies. Scheduling such workflows on heterogeneous edge servers demands simultaneous satisfaction of per workflow deadlines and reliability constraints, since task interruptions or hardware failures on edge devices can cause missed deadlines and degraded service quality. Existing schedulers address latency or throughput but ignore hardware reliability, leaving concurrent agentic pipelines vulnerable. In this paper, we propose DARCS, a Deadline and Reliability Constrained Scheduling framework for LLM agentic workflows at the edge. We formalise the scheduling problem as a combinatorial optimisation whose objective is to maximise the number of accepted (schedulable) workflows subject to task precedence, deadline, reliability, and memory constraints. The model is further extended with load-dependent server failure rates, correlated environmental shocks, and a link-level failure term, all of which integrate as linear drop-in extensions of the core reliability constraint. We derive an Integer Linear Programming (ILP) formulation for exact solutions on small instances. For practical deployments, we propose DARCS-H, a three-phase polynomial-time heuristic that computes reliability-adjusted task priorities, assigns tasks to servers while tracking the remaining reliability budget, and repairs any constraint violation through targeted task reassignment. Simulation experiments with up to twelve concurrent workflows demonstrate that DARCS-H improves Schedulability Ratio under concurrent, reliability-constrained edge workloads by up to ∼54% in the best case and ∼31% on average over related baselines.
ER  - 

