AB  - Dynamic binary translation (DBT) systems still rely heavily on costly hand-written translation rules, making rule construction a major bottleneck when retargeting DBT to new ISAs. ISA manuals and execution feedback provide complementary signals for addressing this challenge. Manuals offer authoritative descriptions of instruction behavior and guide how rules should be constructed, while execution feedback supplies architecture-grounded evidence for checking whether synthesized rules reproduce observable architectural effects. By coupling manual-guided construction with execution-grounded checking, LEMU closes the loop for instruction translation and supports iterative rule synthesis and repair. We present LEMU, a large language model-based framework that operationalizes this workflow to generate compilable QEMU-ready translation rules and iteratively check and repair them through execution feedback. We evaluate LEMU on three representative embedded ISA styles: an accumulator-centric DSP (C28x), a VLIW DSP (C66x), and a 32-bit RISC ISA (ORBIS32). Under a primary repair budget of 10 rounds, the full execution-grounded loop passes 613 of 721 unit tests (85.0%). The synthesized rules also generalize beyond the unit suite, achieving 34/38 (89.5%) on held-out vendor functional suites, 207/234 (88.5%) on TI dsplib for TI ISAs, and 76/90 (84.4%) on PolyBench across all three targets. On Embench, the generated translators support benchmark-level execution; these wall-clock results indicate executability and coarse runtime behavior rather than cycle-accurate or micro-architectural fidelity.
