AB  - Closed-source Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) exhibit strong vision–language reasoning capabilities, yet their robustness under transferable adversarial attacks remains insufficiently understood. Existing black-box attacks mainly rely on pixel-level perturbations, spatial semantic alignment, or latent-space manipulation, but often treat frequency-domain cues and semantic alignment separately. In this work, we propose Frequency–Spatial–Semantic Alignment Attack (FSS-Attack), a transferable targeted attack framework that couples frequency-domain preconditioning with hierarchical spatial-semantic alignment. FSS-Attack first uses a sparsity-controlled Frequency-Domain Pre-Perturbation (FDP) module to construct a frequency-preconditioned anchor in mid-frequency bands, and then applies Multi-Scale Cropping and Hierarchical Ensemble Alignment (MSC-HEA) to optimize target-oriented residual perturbations by aligning global scene structure and local object details across heterogeneous surrogate encoders. Extensive experiments on commercial and open-source MLLMs show that FSS-Attack achieves stronger targeted transferability than representative semantic-alignment and diffusion-based baselines while maintaining competitive perceptual quality. Further analyses, including threshold robustness, fine-grained ablations, frequency-band selection, frequency intervention, and defense evaluation, support the effectiveness of sparse mid-frequency preconditioning and hierarchical semantic alignment. These results suggest that frequency–semantic interactions constitute an important vulnerability surface for multimodal foundation models. Code will be made publicly available upon publication.
