AB  - The widespread adoption of face recognition-based biometric systems has raised significant concerns regarding their vulnerability to Presentation Attacks (PAs), thereby increasing the importance of robust Presentation Attack Detection (PAD) mechanisms. In recent years, Face Recognition Systems (FRSs) have achieved remarkable progress and are now extensively deployed in numerous real-world applications, including smartphone authentication, access control, financial services, and law enforcement. However, despite their effectiveness, these systems remain highly vulnerable to spoofing attacks because attackers can easily obtain facial images or videos from social media platforms, surveillance footage, or other publicly accessible sources without requiring the victim’s cooperation or physical presence. To address these security threats, effective face anti-spoofing techniques must be capable of detecting various attack scenarios, including printed photo attacks, replay attacks, and 3D mask attacks. This survey provides a comprehensive review and critical analysis of existing face PAD approaches, covering traditional handcrafted-feature-based methods, deep-learning-based techniques, and hybrid frameworks. Furthermore, we present a detailed taxonomy of face spoofing attacks and analyze the strengths and limitations of state-of-the-art PAD methods across different datasets, evaluation protocols, and performance metrics. In addition, this survey highlights current challenges affecting the generalization and robustness of face anti-spoofing systems, including domain adaptation, cross-dataset evaluation, illumination variations, and emerging attack techniques. Finally, we discuss promising future research directions aimed at improving the reliability, scalability, and real-world deployment of secure face recognition systems.
