KW  - Token embedding manifold space
KW  - Clustering
AB  - While Greedy Coordinate Gradient (GCG) attacks are effective at bypassing Large Language Model (LLM) safety alignments, they remain limited by constructing adversarial suffixes at the discrete token level. Existing methods ignore the global geometric structure of the low-dimensional token embedding manifold, resulting in suffixes with poor semantic coherence and a tendency to fall into local optima. This severely restricts both attack success rates and convergence efficiency. Addressing this limitation, our multi-method clustering analysis reveals exploitable structural vulnerabilities within the manifold space. Leveraging these insights, we propose Clustered Greedy Coordinate Gradient (C-GCG), a framework that utilizes manifold geometric distribution characteristics to craft precise, semantically coherent adversarial suffixes. Evaluations show that C-GCG significantly outperforms the original GCG baseline in convergence speed, transferability, and overall efficacy. Notably, under data-scarce conditions (120 samples), C-GCG improves the attack success rate by 11.6 percentage points (a 35.7% relative increase). By shifting the jailbreaking paradigm from discrete token selection to global geometric computation within the embedding manifold, this work establishes a robust new direction for LLM security research.
