AB  - Embodied intelligence (EI) integrates Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) and Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) into their perception stacks, combining low-level visual recognition with high-level semantic reasoning. This hybrid design, however, demands rigorous adversarial evaluation to uncover joint vulnerabilities and guide effective defenses. Existing attacks typically target either DNNs or LVLMs in isolation, failing to generalize across the full perception stack, thereby limiting their applicability in security scenarios. This paper presents UniSub, a unified subspace-based black-box adversarial attack designed to simultaneously attack DNNs and LVLMs in EI perception models. UniSub distinguishes itself by: (1) transferability - aligning adversarial subspaces across diverse surrogate models to capture universally exploitable gradients, significantly improving attack success rates on unseen models; (2) efficiency - restricting perturbation optimization to a PCA-derived low-dimensional subspace for faster convergence without sacrificing performance; (3) reproducibility - integrating a differentiable renderer and causal prompt generator to fuse perturbations with semantically valid backgrounds, ensuring persistent adversarial effects across physical realizations. Extensive experiments show that UniSub significantly improves transferability (with higher ASR ∼×1.37 in digital) and reproducibility (with higher ASR ∼×2.14 in physical). This work exposes emerging vulnerabilities in EIs’ perception models and offers a practical framework for unified adversarial testing.
