DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2026.108195
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950584926001849
KW  - Fuzzing
KW  - Vulnerability detection
KW  - Software testing
KW  - Deep learning libraries
AB  - Context:
In recent years, fuzzing techniques for deep learning libraries have been proposed. Although these techniques can detect vulnerabilities, they exhibit notable limitations. Existing methods either rely on blackbox testing mechanisms to generate inputs, leading to limited input effectiveness, or only provide runtime coverage feedback through the C++ frontend, while overlooking vulnerability detection in the Python frontend, which represents the primary usage scenario.
Objective:
We propose Probe, a coverage-guided fuzzing approach for deep learning libraries. The objectives are mainly: (1) to enable runtime coverage feedback for testing via the Python frontend, thereby facilitating vulnerability detection in mainstream usage scenarios; and (2) to generate diverse and edge-case inputs for more comprehensive testing.
Methods:
First, we design a function-level initialization instrumentation strategy to provide coverage feedback for testing through the Python frontend. Second, we incorporate both diverse and edge-case mutation strategies to generate effective test inputs. In addition, we perform key-element position analysis on seeds to guide mutation position selection. Finally, we leverage large language models to repair invalid seed samples, enabling further exploration.
Results:
We implement a prototype of Probe and evaluate it on PyTorch and TensorFlow. Compared with five state-of-the-art fuzzing tools targeting the Python frontend, Probe improves overall edge coverage by 38% to 342% and increases the vulnerability detection rate by 1.5 to 5.0 times. In addition, Probe detects 21 previously unknown vulnerabilities, 19 of which have been confirmed by developers.
Conclusion:
The experimental results demonstrate that Probe achieves superior performance, highlighting the effectiveness of the proposed approach.
ER  - 

