DA  - 2026/11/01/
SN  - 0950-5849
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2026.108288
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950584926002776
KW  - Code agent
KW  - LLM agent security
KW  - Prompt injection
KW  - Model context protocol
KW  - Supply-chain attack
KW  - Sandbox escape
KW  - Focused survey
AB  - Context:
Code agents bind Large Language Models (LLMs) to shells, files, Model Context Protocol (MCP) tools, and long-term memory, converting untrusted semantics into actionable capability and shifting risk from harmful text to actionable harm: remote code execution, zero-click exfiltration, and persistent backdoors.
Objectives:
This work characterizes the attack surfaces, defenses, and evaluation gaps that distinguish code agents from general-purpose LLM agents, and synthesizes them into a reproducible reference for follow-up research and practice.
Methods:
A focused-survey protocol covers eight sources (ACM, IEEE, USENIX, arXiv, NVD-CVE, vendor advisories) over 2024–2026. From 842 initial entries, 82 papers are selected via a PRISMA-inspired pipeline with three-stage Cohen’s κ of 0.74, 0.81, 0.86. Attacks, defenses, and benchmarks are coded along a two-layer taxonomy and a four-stage Prevention/Detection/Containment/Recovery (PDCR) framework.
Results:
(i) A two-layer taxonomy (5 surfaces × 6 attributes) uses attacker-artifact entry into the trusted execution chain as a single-point decidable criterion. (ii) Four cross-layer attack chains are induced: Toxic Agent Flow, Skill-RCE, Zero-click Markdown (EchoLeak), Train-to-Production. (iii) Eight defenses are restructured along PDCR, revealing stage mismatch, surface bias, and unverified adaptive robustness. (iv) For eight benchmarks, coverage matrices show one-eighth covering memory, none covering supply chain, one-eighth including adaptive baselines, and mean attribute coverage 3.1 out of 6.
Conclusion:
Attack evolution, defense coverage, and evaluation are systematically mismatched: AIShellJack reports Attack Success Rates of 83.4% on Cursor Auto Mode and 41.1% on GitHub Copilot in real IDEs. Five priorities are proposed: formal attack-chain analysis, MCP capability attestation, multi-agent information flow control, security-aware evaluation, and end-to-end supply-chain security.
ER  - 

