AB  - Smart contracts, as self-executing blockchain programs, require pre-deployment security audits that primarily integrate automated vulnerability detection and manual review. However, the widespread lack or inconsistency of comments in smart contracts, often resulting from code cloning and poor documentation, hinders code understanding and compromises security auditing. Consequently, comment generation has become a key issue in ensuring smart contract security. Traditional approaches (i.e., information retrieval-based and neural network-based), relying on shallow semantic matching, fail to capture the complex logic and context of smart contracts. Recently, large language models (LLMs) offer promising advances through strong contextual understanding, but existing approaches remain constrained by the quality of dataset, limited contextual awareness and domain knowledge integration during reasoning, and evaluation practices that overemphasize surface-level textual similarity. To address these limitations, we propose SIRCOT, a novel LLM-based prompting approach for smart contract comment generation. It first constructs a high-quality dataset that incorporated an information retrieval mechanism to provide rich syntactic and semantic expression patterns, then introduces Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting to enable deep contextual inference, and summarizes scenario-specific knowledge to guide focused reasoning. Furthermore, the evaluation of SIRCOT additionally incorporates semantic similarity. Results demonstrate that SIRCOT outperforms state-of-the-art baselines across textual similarity metrics, achieving improvements of 3.82%, 5.64%, and 4.27% in BLEU-4, METEOR, and ROUGE-L, respectively, as well as improvements of 5.39% and 2.07% on semantic similarity metrics SBCS and SIDE. Moreover, ablation studies confirm the effectiveness of SIRCOT’s key phases, while both human and tool-based evaluations further show its superior performance.
