KW  - Transferability
KW  - Vision-language models
AB  - Face recognition systems (FRS) have achieved remarkable progress in real-world applications but they remain vulnerable to adversarial examples. Such attacks manipulate benign face images to mislead deployed FRS, posing risks to identity authentication, financial assets, and personal security. However, existing face attack methods often transfer poorly across models and insufficiently exploit target-identity semantics, especially in impersonation attack tasks. To address these limitations, we propose S2FA, a transferable multimodal framework for face impersonation attacks that integrates semantic guidance with a scaled model ensemble strategy. S2FA employs a diffusion-based generator conditioned on textual prompts and visual features to produce semantically guided adversarial face candidates. A Grad-CAM-based semantic projector further localizes identity-relevant regions and constrains perturbation injection, while a scaled surrogate ensemble strategy improves cross-model transferability. These components are jointly optimized through a multi-objective loss that combines adversarial embedding similarity, CLIP-based semantic alignment, and perceptual regularization, thereby improving attack success while preserving visual fidelity. Experiments on benchmark datasets and multiple FRS show that S2FA outperforms state-of-the-art methods, achieving up to a 20% improvement in attack success rate in the best case. Ablation studies and additional analyses further confirm the contribution of each component.
