AB  - This paper presents a systematic literature review (SLR) on DevAIOps — the integration of explainable AI agents and Large Language Models (LLMs) into DevOps and DevSecOps workflows. Following PRISMA, 222 candidate publications from Scopus and Web of Science and manually identified seminal works were screened to 217 unique records, 47 eligible studies, and ten high-impact studies (2019–2025) synthesized in depth across AIOps, CI/CD optimization, DevSecOps, explainable AI, and LLM agents for autonomous remediation. Guided by three research questions on AI techniques, explainability, and security in DevOps automation, the synthesis reveals four critical gaps: the absence of a unified DevAIOps architecture, the absence of standardized KPI benchmarks (notably MTTR), the lack of agent-level explainability adapted to pipelines, and the under-studied risk of LLM hallucinations in autonomous remediation aligned with the OWASP Top 10 for LLM and for Agentic AI Applications (2026). For the two trust gaps, a trust loop is proposed in which artifact-linked explanations feed OWASP-aligned multi-layer validation and risk-tiered human-in-the-loop gating before any remediation is applied. For each gap, concrete research directions are formulated toward trustworthy, explainable, and secure DevAIOps.
