AB  - This paper proposes the concept of an extended psychological profile and substantiates the importance of including empathy as a core factor in establishing trust between a human and an artificial empathetic partner. The global objective of this study is to investigate the psychological mechanisms of trust formation in the digital environment and to evaluate human vulnerability to empathetic impacts. To achieve this, we utilize two complementary experimental platforms modeling different communication channels: the social-emotional game “Teleport” (establishing trust through collaborative non-verbal actions) and the “Virtual Hotel Receptionist” system (establishing trust through verbal dialogue based on the eBICA cognitive architecture and language models). The paper systematically addresses three research tasks aligned with specific measurement approaches: the connection between empathy metrics and stable personality traits is determined via correlation analysis of the Boyko, Mehrabian, and Cattell 16PF scales; the effectiveness of structured dialogue with an empathetic partner in facilitating trust formation and fostering social contact is evaluated via a pre-post testing design using the Artificial Social Agent (ASA) questionnaire; the influence of empathy channels on subjective perception and partner evaluation under severe information deficits is verified through a blind indistinguishable setup in the gaming environment. The obtained results allow for modeling both pro-social interaction and human vulnerability to digital manipulations, including potential cyber-fraud scenarios.
