KW  - Large language models
KW  - Secure web scraping
KW  - Retrieval-augmented generation
KW  - Hypothetical document embeddings
KW  - Implicit parameter reconstruction
KW  - Execution-grounded code generation
AB  - Automating interactions with security-sensitive web services remains difficult due to dynamic frontends, session-bound tokens, and undocumented parameter-generation logic. We propose ASG-LLM, an execution-grounded pipeline that generates scraping modules from a single successful browser session trace. ASG-LLM captures full HTTP(S) traffic, removes irrelevant requests through multi-stage filtering, and constructs a dependency-aware representation of the core interaction flow. When security-critical parameters cannot be reconstructed from trace-derived evidence, ASG-LLM distinguishes between organization-owned internal functions and externally delivered JavaScript dependencies. FUNC Retriever uses Hypothetical Document Embeddings (HyDE) to identify reusable internal functions and provides the LLM with abstraction-level documentation without exposing proprietary implementations. Where the generation logic instead resides in an external script loaded during the session, that resource is analyzed separately to identify the relevant transformation behavior. Generated modules are executed in a controlled environment, with structured failure feedback for targeted revision. ASG-LLM was evaluated through case studies on five anonymized security-sensitive web services. The retrieval evaluation comprised ten dynamic-parameter cases, two per service. HyDE-guided selective retrieval produced more exact Top-1 and Top-3 reference-function matches than the all-functions-as-context baseline. In a separate constrained same-session replay evaluation using captured dynamic values, the generated modules completed 11 of 15 authenticated workflow trials. This provides preliminary evidence of module executability under captured-value conditions but does not establish fresh-session dynamic-parameter regeneration or full retrieval-integrated execution reliability. The results suggest that combining trace grounding, selective retrieval, and execution-time validation is a promising direction for web automation in security-constrained environments.
