KW  - Persistent memory
KW  - Generative AI
KW  - Psychological dependence
KW  - Corporate governance
KW  - Risk allocation
KW  - Strategic solvency
AB  - Memory-augmented generative AI binds users to systems that retain tone, disclosures and vulnerability across sessions, so psychological dependence becomes a structural condition of engagement. Organizations stabilize and capitalize this reliance, while governance frameworks continue to treat persistent memory as a technical configuration detached from exposure and value. The article develops the Dependence Solvency Model (DSM) to classify deployments that revise dependence capital and internal control when severe outcomes emerge and to distinguish them from trajectories that absorb escalating harm as operational overhead. The framework articulates DSM both as a diagnostic classification and as a preventive governance architecture that introduces disclosure sequences, oversight roles and supervisory channels for board evaluation. The analysis integrates the technical properties of decision-based and evaluation-based memory, examines how adverse uptake and DSM-washing alter governance signals, and identifies the external ecosystem that shapes organizational judgement. The article closes by outlining conceptual and empirical work required to track escalation patterns and to determine when extreme harm becomes a governance event rather than an indicator of scale.
ER  - 
